{"title":"2026 BmMT Individual Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_BmMT_Individual_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>How many ways are there to order the letters in “BMMT” such that the two M’s are next to each other? For example, two such orderings are “BMMT” and “TMMB.”\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_BmMT_Individual_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, we must find the positions the 2 M's can be in. There are 3 ways, listed below. \n</p><p>MM__\n</p><p>_MM_\n</p><p>__MM\n</p><p>In each case, there are 2 ways to place the B, as they can go in either empty position, and the T fits into place. \n</p><p>Therefore, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/7/b97ac3d6052b56dbdc7cac9881383a38b5968b5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\cdot 3 = 6$\" width=\"64\" height=\"12\" /> ways\n</p><p>~Solution and formatting by xue1\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018034502\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.005 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.006 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 8/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 155/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,